yes, I think this is correct. the DIC follows the original DIC paper on the
p.eff issue, although p.eff can be estimated another ways, some to be known to
be positive.

overall, WAIC is a more sound criteria, so...

>    2.

>       If so, is there a way to compute the effective number of parameters for
> DIC using the variance of the score, similar to WAIC?

or simply us WAIC instead?  there is little gain in changing the p.eff
calculations in DIC, as WAIC is kind of superior

>    3.

>       Does a negative effective number of parameters in DIC necessarily
> indicate a problem in the model, such as overfitting, non-identifiability, or
> numerical instability due to copied components or something related to random
> effects?

there is a discussion of this issue in the original DIC paper, and I refer to
that one.

