In-Reply-To: <JEqHPaju6QQ.KmutpzXoAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <JEqHPaju6QQ.j4llPsbcBQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com> <JEqHPaju6QQ.KmutpzXoAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/JEqHPaju6QQ/m/Lyz6Z4jBBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Like I said before,

If your inla output gives you a vector that contains the values for each vertex, but for each multinomial alternative, you'll need to extract the relevant subset of the vector. The indexing order should be either (1:n, 1:n, ...), or (1, 1, 1, ..., 2, 2, 2, ...), so either
  [seq_len(fm_dof(mesh)) + fm_dof(mesh) * (alternative - 1)]
or
  [alternative + num_alternatives * (seq_len(fm_dof(mesh)) - 1)]

The indexing is applied to whatever part of the inla model produces the values at the mesh vertices, and the ordering depends on how it's setup; presumably the rows of your predictor stack, if that's how you setup the model. If you just want the $summary.random$yourcomponentname$mean, that's the easiest, as you don't need a prediction stack for that.

Try both orderings; it should be obvious which one is correct, as the wrong one would lead to nonsensical spatial patterns.

Finn

